  <dashboards>
    <dashboard enable-sort-zone-taborder='true' name='Part 1: Theoretical Questions'>
      <style />
      <size maxheight='800' maxwidth='1000' minheight='800' minwidth='1000' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='4' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98000' id='3' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='1000'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Part 1: Theoretical Questions</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 1: What is a Calculated Field and why is it important in business dashboards?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> A Calculated Field allows you to create new data from the existing data in your data source using formulas. It is crucial for business dashboards because it enables you to derive custom KPIs (like Profit Margin or Year-over-Year Growth), segment data conditionally (like grouping ages into buckets), and perform advanced analytics without needing to alter the underlying database.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 2: Why can&apos;t parameters change visuals directly in Tableau?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> A parameter is simply an independent, global placeholder value (like a number, date, or string). On its own, it doesn&apos;t &quot;do&quot; anything. To actually change a visual, a parameter </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>must</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> be connected to a Calculated Field, a Filter, or a Reference Line. The calculation or filter acts as the engine that applies the parameter&apos;s value to the data on the dashboard.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 3: When should EXCLUDE LOD be used?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> An </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Courier New'>EXCLUDE</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Level of Detail (LOD) expression is used when you want to calculate a value at a </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>higher</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> level of detail than what is currently displayed in your view. For example, if your chart displays sales by State, but you want to calculate the total sales for the entire Region to figure out the State&apos;s percentage of the regional total, you would use </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Courier New'>EXCLUDE [State]</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> to ignore the state-level granularity.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 4: Difference between FIXED and INCLUDE LOD expressions?</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>FIXED LOD:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Calculates the value using </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>only</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> the specific dimensions you list in the formula, completely ignoring whatever dimensions are currently in the visual view (unless a context filter is applied). It is absolute.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>INCLUDE LOD:</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Calculates the value using the specific dimensions you list in the formula </run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>in addition</run>
              <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> to whatever dimensions are currently in the visual view. It is relative to the view.</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <devicelayouts>
        <devicelayout auto-generated='true' name='Phone'>
          <size maxheight='700' minheight='700' sizing-mode='vscroll' />
          <zones>
            <zone h='100000' id='6' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
              <zone h='98000' id='5' param='vert' type-v2='layout-flow' w='98400' x='800' y='1000'>
                <zone h='98000' id='3' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='1000'>
                  <formatted-text>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Part 1: Theoretical Questions</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 1: What is a Calculated Field and why is it important in business dashboards?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> A Calculated Field allows you to create new data from the existing data in your data source using formulas. It is crucial for business dashboards because it enables you to derive custom KPIs (like Profit Margin or Year-over-Year Growth), segment data conditionally (like grouping ages into buckets), and perform advanced analytics without needing to alter the underlying database.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 2: Why can&apos;t parameters change visuals directly in Tableau?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> A parameter is simply an independent, global placeholder value (like a number, date, or string). On its own, it doesn&apos;t &quot;do&quot; anything. To actually change a visual, a parameter </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>must</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> be connected to a Calculated Field, a Filter, or a Reference Line. The calculation or filter acts as the engine that applies the parameter&apos;s value to the data on the dashboard.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 3: When should EXCLUDE LOD be used?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> An </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Courier New'>EXCLUDE</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Level of Detail (LOD) expression is used when you want to calculate a value at a </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>higher</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> level of detail than what is currently displayed in your view. For example, if your chart displays sales by State, but you want to calculate the total sales for the entire Region to figure out the State&apos;s percentage of the regional total, you would use </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Courier New'>EXCLUDE [State]</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> to ignore the state-level granularity.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Question 4: Difference between FIXED and INCLUDE LOD expressions?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Answer:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>FIXED LOD:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Calculates the value using </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>only</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> the specific dimensions you list in the formula, completely ignoring whatever dimensions are currently in the visual view (unless a context filter is applied). It is absolute.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>INCLUDE LOD:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> Calculates the value using the specific dimensions you list in the formula </run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' italic='true'>in addition</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'> to whatever dimensions are currently in the visual view. It is relative to the view.</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
                    <format attr='padding' value='0' />
                  </zone-style>
                </zone>
              </zone>
              <zone-style>
                <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                <format attr='margin' value='8' />
              </zone-style>
            </zone>
          </zones>
        </devicelayout>
      </devicelayouts>
      <simple-id uuid='{11B18CA3-D083-49B7-8A1B-B8E53E942336}' />
    </dashboard>
